From eeb0a41ec9f9551839377aa5835e030a70cf6380 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 14:45:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 208/209] feat(area): distributor chord alignment,
 stretch-to-fit, forward axis

A fence panel is a chord, not a curve. Follow Tangent orients each instance by
the tangent at its OWN sample point, so both of its ends sit off the line and
the chain opens up at every bend - which is the row of short, separated sections
the user photographed.

Alignment gains "Point To Next" (EDistributorAlign::Chord): the instance still
sits on its arc-length sample, but its forward axis aims at where the NEXT
instance starts. The last one aims at the end of the usable curve, and if it is
already there it keeps the tangent. The frame is otherwise the one
CSplineDistributor builds - forward, roll-aware normal, their cross product - so
per-point Angle still banks it and Frame Mode still decides the up vector. The
normal is re-orthogonalised against the chord first, because it is only
perpendicular to the TANGENT; using it raw would shear the frame and the mesh
with it.

Stretch To Fit (off by default, meaningful in Chord) scales the instance along
its forward axis until its length equals the chord, so sections meet end to end
with no gap. It needs the mesh's own extent, read from IStatObj::GetAABB(), and
the scale is applied in MESH space - frame * axis fix * local scale - because
"longer" has to mean longer along the fence, not taller.

Forward Axis (+X default, +Y, -X, -Y) says which axis of the mesh runs along the
curve. +X is the default because that is what the legacy object assumes:
CSplineDistributor builds its matrix with SetFromVectors(tangent, normal,
tangent x normal, pos) (SplineDistributor.cpp:286-293) and SetFromVectors puts
its first argument in column 0, i.e. on local +X. The same .cgf that lined up
under the legacy distributor lines up here untouched; a mesh modelled sideways
now needs a dropdown rather than a re-export.

Pivot At Start (on by default) puts the START FACE of the mesh's bounding box on
the sample point instead of the mesh's own pivot. A section exported around its
centre would otherwise begin half a section before the curve does and leave a
half-section gap at every joint.

The "Count" half of the fit request is a fourth spacing mode, Mesh Length: as
many sections as the mesh's own length tiles into the curve, rounded, with the
residual spread over the spacing - a few per cent of stretch with Stretch To Fit
on, even small gaps without it. With a weighted list of mixed lengths the first
loadable mesh sets the length, which is at least predictable.

Follow Tangent is untouched, and the rebuild log now names the alignment, the
forward axis and whether stretch and pivot-at-start are on.

